First off, I should tell you I'm an extremely picky gamer. And I hate horror games. Still, to say I love Wait-Extended, is an understatement.
The world of Wait-Extended ("Wait" for short) relies on subtlety and suggestion to tell a complex story, suffused with existentialism. There are multiple layers of meaning in the unfolding events, and engrossing sidestories to complement the main plot.
This exploratory adventure builds on its fair share of literary background.  There are even enigmatic easter eggs to be found throughout, which hint at an even bigger, more macabre picture than what you get to see within the storyline itself.
The game appears simple, understated and unassuming at first, and then it slowly but surely gets under your skin. It has you puzzling over its various mysteries long after you've stopped playing, and keeps you coming back for more. In fact, multiple playthroughs are ABSOLUTELY NECESSARY to get the full goodness of this game.
Wait has clearly been lovingly  crafted with incredible attention to detail. Everything you might encounter was put there on purpose. Nothing's been left to chance- if it makes no sense at first, trust that there's a reason why it is that way. If you're considering playing, here's my advice - take nothing for granted. Leave no stone unturned. Prepare to be surprised.
Riddles of varying difficulties drive the game forward and provide the bulk of the challenges. This definitely required for me to think in unexpected ways.
The fear factor: Wait has been very successful in continuing to scare me, even still after several plays. I'm sure this varies from person to person based on tolerance, however, I'd say the designers have done a great job creating an eerie and creepy atmosphere. Soundtrack and sound effects are outstanding. The cuteness of the rpg-maker look gives it an additional layer of interest: some scenes are just adorable (like the protagonist eating dinner or changing for bed) and some scary scenes definitely get scarier because of the creepy-child factor.
*Rave warning*
Last but not least, I must say that my favorite thing about Wait, by far, is the idiosyncratic protagonist, who's in many ways akin to Samsa in The Metamorphosis and to Meursault in The Stranger.
He seems to have no redeeming qualities: he's completely out of touch with reality, boring, resigned, obsessed with hygiene and smells, only goes to the bathroom in the office, engages in problem-solving only reluctantly, avoids asking questions, and to top it all off, he's a hopeless workaholic, who continues to prioritize work in a situation that would send a halfway-sane person running for the hills, AND YET he undeniably manages to capture your heart and have you rooting for him anyway.
*End rave*
Looking forward to many more awesome games from the good people at Rest.less!
In Summary (TL;DR)
Recommended if:
You're in love with complex, multi-layered stories that unfold over time. Even more if you're already a fan of Lovecraft, or surrealist/existential/absurdist literature;
You enjoy breaking your brain figuring out bizarre puzzles;
You're obsessive compulsive and need to search, check and explore everything within a game;
You are willing to put in the time to playthrough multiple times - patience does pay off;
You like to ask questions about the nature of existence and the universe.
Not Recommended if:
You're looking for a super-intense horror experience;
You're wanting lots of action, gore, or fighting scary creatures;
You're not so interested in solving riddles to advance the plot;
You don't plan on playing through more than once;
You want to be told what's going on from the get go, or need your games to come with a tutorial.

